# The red-cabbage rainbow: one pigment, five colours
#
# Cabbage water is not five dyes. It is one pigment that loses protons in
# steps, and each step absorbs light differently — so its colour is a reading
# of the pH. The bench computes it the way it computes every other colour:
# ε(λ) through Beer–Lambert over the CIE observer, not a table of "turns blue
# in alkali".
register lv1

# Vinegar: the flavylium form, red.
add v1 white_vinegar_5_percent 100mL
add v1 red_cabbage_juice 10mL
measure v1 ph
look v1

# Fizzy water, mildly acidic: the middle form, purple. This is the colour
# the jar of juice is before anything is done to it.
new beaker
add v2 sparkling_water 100mL
add v2 red_cabbage_juice 10mL
measure v2 ph
look v2

# Baking soda: the first alkaline step.
new beaker
add v3 water 100mL
add v3 baking_soda 2g
add v3 red_cabbage_juice 10mL
measure v3 ph
look v3

# Washing soda, stronger — and here is the one worth stopping on.
new beaker
add v4 water 100mL
add v4 washing_soda 4g
add v4 red_cabbage_juice 10mL
measure v4 ph
look v4

# Strong alkali, at the top of the ladder.
new beaker
add v5 water 100mL
add v5 NaOH 0.1mol
add v5 red_cabbage_juice 10mL
measure v5 ph
look v5

# No form of this pigment is green. The green appears where a blue form and a
# yellow form are both present: together they absorb at each end of the
# visible range and leave a window between them. It is a colour that is in no
# table in this engine, computed from two that are — which is the whole
# argument for computing colour instead of storing it.
inspect v4

# What this does not model: real cabbage juice is mildly acidic and carries
# its own buffering, so a few drops in unbuffered water would move the pH as
# well as report it; here the juice only reports. The pigment is a mixture of
# acylated cyanidin glycosides rather than one compound, and no cultivar,
# extraction or copigmentation shift is claimed.
